BODY = '<h1>Linode pricing</h1>\n<p><strong>Linode / Akamai Cloud Shared CPU costs $5–$1,152 per month in the North America selection checked below.</strong> The entry Nanode is $5/month or $0.0075/hour; its optional instance-backup plan is $2/month. Extra outbound transfer is $0.005 per billed GB and Block Storage starts at $0.10/GB-month. A powered-off VM still costs money, and its volume can survive deletion. These are USD list rates, before tax and account credits.</p>\n<h2>North America Shared CPU: separate compute and backup rates</h2>\n<p>This is the full Shared CPU table observed in the selected region, with the corresponding Shared CPU backup monthly rate alongside it. Backup prices are plan-specific add-ons, not one percentage applied to every VM. The backup table calls the entry shape “Linode 1 GB”; the compute table calls it “Nanode 1 GB.” Their RAM, CPU, disk and transfer fields match.</p>\n<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Linode Shared CPU prices, scroll horizontally"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Compute USD/mo cap</th><th>Compute USD/hr</th><th>RAM</th><th>CPUs</th><th>Local disk</th><th>Transfer</th><th>Backup USD/mo cap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Nanode 1 GB</th><td>$5.00</td><td>$0.0075</td><td>1 GB</td><td>1</td><td>25 GB</td><td>1 TB</td><td>$2.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 2 GB</th><td>$12.00</td><td>$0.0180</td><td>2 GB</td><td>1</td><td>50 GB</td><td>2 TB</td><td>$2.50</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 4 GB</th><td>$24.00</td><td>$0.0360</td><td>4 GB</td><td>2</td><td>80 GB</td><td>4 TB</td><td>$5.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 8 GB</th><td>$48.00</td><td>$0.0720</td><td>8 GB</td><td>4</td><td>160 GB</td><td>5 TB</td><td>$10.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 16 GB</th><td>$96.00</td><td>$0.1440</td><td>16 GB</td><td>6</td><td>320 GB</td><td>8 TB</td><td>$20.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 32 GB</th><td>$192.00</td><td>$0.2880</td><td>32 GB</td><td>8</td><td>640 GB</td><td>16 TB</td><td>$40.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 64 GB</th><td>$384.00</td><td>$0.5760</td><td>64 GB</td><td>16</td><td>1280 GB</td><td>20 TB</td><td>$80.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 96 GB</th><td>$576.00</td><td>$0.8640</td><td>96 GB</td><td>20</td><td>1920 GB</td><td>20 TB</td><td>$120.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 128 GB</th><td>$768.00</td><td>$1.1520</td><td>128 GB</td><td>24</td><td>2560 GB</td><td>20 TB</td><td>$160.00</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linode 192 GB</th><td>$1,152.00</td><td>$1.7280</td><td>192 GB</td><td>32</td><td>3840 GB</td><td>20 TB</td><td>$240.00</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Data from <a href="https://www.akamai.com/cloud/pricing/cpu">Akamai official CPU pricing → Shared CPU plans → North America</a>, columns Plan, $/Mo, $/Hr, RAM, CPUs, Storage and Transfer; backup column from <a href="https://www.akamai.com/cloud/pricing/backups">official Backups pricing → Shared CPU tab → North America → $/Mo</a>. Checked 2026-11-13 03:54 UTC. Other regions, G8 Dedicated CPU, GPU and additional IPv4 pricing are not interchangeable with this table.</p>\n<h2>Year-one budget, year-two budget, and what prepayment changes</h2>\n<section data-authored-calculation="annual"><p>Our steady-workload scenario holds one Nanode, its instance-backup add-on, and a separately allocated 40 GB volume for twelve complete billed months. The monthly resource budget is $5 + $2 + (40 × $0.10) = $11. <strong>Year one = 12 × $11 = $132. At unchanged rates and resources, year two = $132, so the modelled difference is $0.</strong> Quantities and the unchanged-rate assumption are ours. This is neither a discounted first-year quote nor a guaranteed renewal tariff; a guaranteed future tariff was <strong>Not obtained</strong>.</p>\n<p>A prepayment changes account balance, not the number of resource-months in this model. The official Billing FAQ says advance funds become a positive balance applied to later invoices. Do not count a deposit as an additional annual service fee, and do not assume it buys a renewal discount.</p>\n<p>The $2 backup add-on does not protect the 40 GB Block Storage volume. The official Block Storage documentation explicitly excludes volumes from the instance Backups Service. Our $132 budget therefore covers the listed services, not a complete backup design for every byte. The cost of a separate volume-backup destination was <strong>Not obtained</strong> and is not invented.</p>\n<p>Evidence: the official compute and backup cells above; <a href="https://www.akamai.com/cloud/pricing/block-storage">Block Storage pricing, opening rate sentence and North America 40 GB row</a>; <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/billing-faqs">Billing FAQ → Can I make a payment in advance?, paragraph 1</a>; <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/block-storage">Block Storage → Limits and considerations, final backup-exclusion paragraph</a>.</p></section>\n<h2>A pooled-transfer calculation that keeps the units honest</h2>\n<section data-authored-calculation="pool"><p>Akamai\'s billing guide says compute network transfer and Block Storage use binary units despite the GB/TB labels: one billed TB contains 1,024 billed GB. Preserve those labels when reconciling the provider\'s invoice; do not silently substitute decimal terabytes.</p>\n<p>Our scenario uses two full-month Nanodes in the same eligible transfer pool, both at the checked North America rate. Each contributes 1 TB. Their assumed outbound usage is 1,300 GB and 900 GB. <strong>Pool allowance = 2 × 1,024 = 2,048 GB. Usage = 1,300 + 900 = 2,200 GB. Overage = max(0, 2,200 − 2,048) × $0.005 = $0.76.</strong> A separate full-month 40 GB volume costs 40 × $0.10 = $4. With two $5 Nanodes, the resource subtotal is $10 + $4 + $0.76 = $14.76, excluding backups and tax.</p>\n<p>This does not mean every instance worldwide shares one allowance. The network-transfer document distinguishes the global pool, region-specific pools and distributed regions. Confirm the actual pool in Cloud Manager; a contribution to another pool cannot offset this example. Short-lived services also contribute a prorated allowance, so the full-month assumption is necessary.</p>\n<p>Evidence: <a href="https://www.akamai.com/cloud/pricing/cpu">Shared CPU → North America, Nanode Transfer cell and following egress-overage sentence</a>; <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/network-transfer-usage-and-costs">Network transfer usage and costs → Transfer allowance, pooling and proration paragraphs</a>; <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/understanding-how-billing-works">Billing → GB vs GiB conversion of bytes, paragraph 1</a>. Traffic quantities are our scenario, not measured usage.</p></section>\n<h2>Shutdown and deletion: the volume is an independent service</h2>\n<section data-authored-calculation="retained-volume"><p>For a later complete billed month, model a Nanode and a 40 GB volume with no backup add-on, no transfer overage and no other resources. <strong>Powered off but retained: $5 + $4 = $9. Nanode already deleted, volume retained: $4. Both resources already deleted: no further charge for these two resources.</strong> The retained volume alone is $4 × 12 = $48 over twelve complete months at the checked rate. A shutdown button therefore does not accomplish the same budget change as deleting the services.</p>\n<p>Amounts already accrued before deletion still appear on the later invoice. The volume documentation says volume data persists after deleting its attached Linode; the Billing FAQ says inactive services remain chargeable until deleted. These statements establish the separate cleanup check. They do not promise a refund for elapsed time or describe the deletion policy of every optional service.</p>\n<p>Evidence: <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/billing-faqs">Billing FAQ → I\'m not using services but I\'m still being charged, paragraph 1; I deleted a service earlier this month, paragraph 1</a>; <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/block-storage">Block Storage → Resilient and fault tolerant, paragraph 1</a>; <a href="https://www.akamai.com/cloud/pricing/block-storage">North America 40 GB monthly row</a>.</p></section>\n<h2>Short-lived jobs: use the rounded hour count</h2>\n<section data-authored-calculation="rounding"><p>For a Nanode that exists for 24 hours and one minute, our billable-time calculation is ceil(24 + 1/60) = 25 hours. At the displayed $0.0075/hour, the unrounded arithmetic result is <strong>25 × $0.0075 = $0.1875</strong>, below the $5 monthly cap. Exactly 24 hours gives 24 × $0.0075 = $0.18. The extra partial hour adds $0.0075 before invoice rounding. We have not obtained the account\'s final currency-rounding result, so $0.1875 is a formula result, not a claim about a four-decimal invoice.</p>\n<p>Evidence: <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/understanding-how-billing-works">Billing → Understand how billing works, service-accrual paragraph: usage is rounded up to the nearest hour</a>; <a href="https://www.akamai.com/cloud/pricing/cpu">Nanode $/Hr and $/Mo cells</a>. The runtime is our assumed job duration. A job\'s transfer allowance must be prorated separately.</p></section>\n<h2>Who this fits, and what remains unquoted</h2>\n<p>Use this guide for Shared CPU workloads whose bursty CPU needs fit a shared plan. Check a different family when you need dedicated CPU consistency; the capped rates here do not describe every Akamai product. Treat storage capacity and storage protection as two separate decisions. Adding a volume solves one and leaves the other for your backup design.</p>\n<ul><li>Guaranteed future renewal rate: <strong>Not obtained</strong>; the two-year budget explicitly holds rates constant.</li><li>Separate volume-backup destination and standalone snapshot/image-storage quote: <strong>Not obtained</strong>; neither is included in the budget.</li><li>Account-specific credits, tax-inclusive checkout total and final invoice rounding: <strong>Not obtained</strong>.</li><li>Other-region prices: <strong>Not obtained for this guide</strong>; North America is the selected scope.</li></ul>\n<h2>Official sources and check date</h2>\n<p>Official price cards checked 2026-11-13 03:54 UTC. Price tables were read in the public browser with North America and Shared CPU selected; they were not imported from a third-party comparison site. These are dated editorial checks, not a live price feed. The billing and storage documents are linked to named sections beside each calculation.</p>\n<p><a href="/">Home</a> · <a href="/compare/">Compare published plans</a> · <a href="/provider-guides/linode/">Linode / Akamai source index</a></p>\n'
HUB = '<section class="page-head"><h1>Linode / Akamai Cloud source index</h1><p>North America Shared CPU observations, checked 2026-11-13 03:54 UTC. Read the <a href="/pricing/linode/">Linode pricing guide</a> for the full price ladder and worked resource budgets.</p></section><section class="prose"><h2>Published starting configurations</h2><ul><li>Nanode 1 GB: $5/month cap, $0.0075/hour, 25 GB local storage, 1 TB transfer.</li><li>Linode 2 GB: $12/month cap, $0.0180/hour, 50 GB local storage, 2 TB transfer.</li><li>Linode 4 GB: $24/month cap, $0.0360/hour, 80 GB local storage, 4 TB transfer.</li></ul><p>Source: <a href="https://www.akamai.com/cloud/pricing/cpu">official CPU pricing, Shared CPU table, North America selection</a>. These are USD list rates; other locations and product families require separate checks.</p><h2>Billing scope to verify</h2><p>The <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/billing-faqs">Billing FAQ</a> distinguishes a stopped service from a deleted one. The <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/block-storage">Block Storage documentation</a> explains that an attached volume survives deleting its Linode and is not covered by instance backups. The <a href="https://techdocs.akamai.com/cloud-computing/docs/network-transfer-usage-and-costs">network transfer guide</a> describes which allowance pool applies.</p><p>Our article turns those rules into annual, excess-transfer and retained-volume calculations. Assumed usage is labelled as a scenario; guaranteed future renewal prices were not obtained.</p><p><a href="/pricing/linode/">Open the complete Linode guide</a> · <a href="/">Home</a> · <a href="/compare/">Compare</a></p></section>'
